{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>If a soul commits a trespass and sins unwittingly against the holy things of HaShem.</b> Here, in the passage about consecrated property, he placed מעילה (trespass) before חטא (sin); but in the passage about deposit and matters between a man and his fellow, he placed חטא before מעילה — as it says, ״נפש כי תחטא ומעלה מעל בה׳״ — and similarly here he says וחמישיתו, and in the passage about deposit, וחמישיתיו. The reason for all this is that the term מעילה denotes an intentional act, as they said (Ezra 9:2): ״וד השרים והסגנים היתה במעל הזה ראשונה,״ whereas the term חטא denotes unwitting action, from the language of (Judges 20) ״ולא יחטיא.״ Therefore, in the case of consecrated property, which falls under the statutes of the Holy One, blessed be He, he placed מעילה before חטא — and the matter there is unwitting, and therefore he used the term חטא, which is the unwitting. But in the statutes governing human beings, in the claims between a man and his fellow, he placed חטא before מעילה — and the matter there is intentional, and therefore he mentioned מעילה last. And all this is from the loving-kindness of the Holy One, blessed be He, for He was more stringent in the statutes governing human beings, between one and another, than He was in His own statute, blessed be He." "<b>And behold,</b> this is a great principle in language: there is an enormous difference between words when the later is placed first and the earlier placed last, even though it may appear that all is one and makes no difference. Thus it is written (I Kings 20:20): ״תפשום חיים״ and it says ״חיים תפשום,״ and this is what Ben-Hadad king of Aram said concerning the people who went out from Samaria: if they went out in peace, ״תפשום חיים״ — and if for war, ״חיים תפשום.״ The meaning of ״תפשום חיים״ is: seize them and leave them alive — that is, do not kill them; whereas the meaning of ״חיים תפשום״ is that he commanded to kill them and to take their lives from them. And we find in many places that the Holy One, blessed be He, strongly upheld the law governing the conduct of creatures between one and another, and was zealous for their honor more than for His own honor. For thus we find concerning Abraham, peace be upon him (Genesis 12:3): ״ומקללך אאור״ — He ascribed the vengeance to Himself, blessed be He, and said ״אאור״; but concerning His own honor He said (I Samuel 2:30): ״כי מכבדי אכבד ובוזי יקלו,״ and did not say אקל. And similarly in the matter of Jeroboam: when he was offering to idolatry, his hand did not wither; but when he slighted the honor of the prophet and sent his hand against him, his hand withered — as it is written (I Kings 13:4): ״וישלח ירבעם את ידו מעל המזבח לאמר תפשוהו ותיבש ידו אשר שלח אליו ולא יכול להשיבה אליו.״ You thereby learn that the Holy One, blessed be He, was zealous for the honor of His prophets more than for His own honor. And similarly with the army of the king of Aram: He did not strike them with blindness when they came against the Holy One, blessed be He — for they would go to the houses of their abominations to worship their idols — but when they came against Elisha to seize him, it is written (II Kings 6:18): ״ויכם בסנורים כדבר אלישע.״ And what is written ״וחמישיתיו״ in the passage about deposit may be explained thus: after he sinned and dealt falsely with his fellow concerning a deposit or an assignment, and added transgression to his sin by swearing falsely about it, it is fitting that he add many fifths. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that the intent is not to pay many fifths at once, but rather to say that if he stubbornly refused and did not wish to give the fifth, he becomes liable to pay many fifths on account of the refusal — because he did not wish to pay a single fifth. <br>And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted <b>If a soul sins and commits a trespass against HaShem</b>: R. Akiva said — what is the force of ״ומעלה מעל בה׳״? Because all who lend and borrow do so only with witnesses and a document; therefore when one denies, he denies the witnesses and the document. But one who deposits with his fellow does not want any person to know, only the third party between them — therefore when he denies, he denies the third party between them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:11.343691483Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}